{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["These are the generations of Jacob — let those who love insight understand and know all that our Rabbis taught us, that no verse leaves the hand of its plain sense. Even though the essence of the Torah comes to teach us and make known to us by way of the implication of the peshat and the aggadot and the halakhot and the laws, and by way of extended expression, and by way of the thirty-two rules of Rabbi Eliezer son of Rabbi Yose HaGelili, and by way of the thirteen rules of Rabbi Yishmael — and the early ones, out of their piety, engaged in inclining toward the derashot, which are the essence — and as a result they were not accustomed to the depth of the plain sense of Scripture. For the Sages said: do not multiply logic for your children. And they also said: one who engages in Scripture — it is a measure and not a measure; one who engages in Talmud — there is no measure greater than this. And as a result they were not so accustomed to the plain sense of verses, as we say in tractate Shabbat: I was eighteen years old and I studied the entire Talmud, and I did not know that no verse leaves the hand of its plain sense. And also Rabbeinu Shelomo, my mother's father, the light of the eyes of the exile, who interpreted Torah, Prophets, and Writings, set his mind to interpret the plain sense of Scripture. And I, Shmuel son of R. Meir, his son-in-law, of blessed memory — I debated with him, and in his presence, and he acknowledged to me that had he had leisure, he would have needed to make other interpretations according to the peshatot that are newly discovered every day. And now the discerning ones will see what the early ones interpreted." "These are the generations of Jacob — these are the verses and the events that happened to Jacob. But this is vanity, for all instances of אלה תולדות mentioned in the Torah and in the Writings — some of them explicitly mean sons, and some of them explicitly mean grandsons, as I explained in 'אלה תולדות נח'. Above, in the portion of Genesis, it is written: ויהי נח בן חמש מאות שנה ויולד נח את שם את חם ואת יפת. And afterward it explains that the world sinned: ונח מצא חן. And after that it explains: אלה תולדות נח — his grandsons, how so, for he had three sons and the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded him to bring them into the ark for twelve months, and when they came out they had sons born to them after the flood, until they total seventy sons, which were the seventy nations, as it is written: מאלה נפרדו וגו׳. And likewise with Esav: the first section explains Esav's sons who were born to him in his father's place, and after that: וילך אל ארץ וגו׳ וישב לו בהר שעיר, and the entire section. And it is written: אלה תולדות עשו אבי אדום בהר שעיר. And this entire second section is about Esav's sons. And just as we find with Esav, where it explained that his sons were born in his father's dwelling place before he went to another land on account of Jacob, and his grandsons were born in Mount Seir — so too with Jacob: above it is written: ויהיו בני יעקב שנים עשר וגו׳. And it explains at the end: אלה בני יעקב אשר ילדו לו בפדן ארם ויבא אל יצחק אביו וגו׳ — there, it explained Jacob's sons and where they were born, just as it did with Esav's sons. And now it writes: אלה תולדות יעקב — his grandsons, who were seventy, and how they were born. How so: Joseph was seventeen years old, and his brothers were jealous of him, and as a result Judah separated from his brothers and had sons in Keziv and in Adullam — Shelah, Peretz, and Zerah. And the matter unfolded such that Joseph was brought down to Egypt, and Manasseh and Ephraim were born to him in Egypt, and Joseph sent for his father and his household, until they were seventy. And all this Moshe Rabbenu needed to write, for on this basis he reminded them: בשבעים נפש ירדו אבותיך וגו׳." "Ben sheva-esreh shanah — to inform you that he was hidden from his father Jacob for twenty-two years, for he was thirty years old when he stood before Pharaoh, and seven years of plenty, and two years of famine, and then his father came, as it is written: ועוד חמש שנים אשר אין חריש וקציר." "Was tending his brothers with the flock — the sons of Leah; according to the way of the world he called them 'his brothers,' but not the sons of the maidservants." "And he was a lad with the sons of Bilhah, etc. — his youthfulness and his habits and his socializing were with the sons of Bilhah and the sons of Zilpah, and as a result his brothers, the sons of Leah, began to hate him." "Et eḥav — with his brothers he was a shepherd, and in the high spirits of his youth he kept apart from them and associated with the sons of the maidservants and not with them, like a na'ar. As it is written: כי נער ישראל. And it is written: יקומו נא הנערים וישחקו לפנינו. The text goes on enumerating all kinds of matters that caused them to hate him. And also —" "And Yosef brought evil report of them — the evil report of his brothers. In accordance with the derash of Aggadah according to my interpretation: he said to his father, 'Thus do the sons of the maidservants demean them, but I honor them and I associate with them.' Other peshat interpreters did not grasp the essential point."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {0 "זצ״ל after Rashbam's own name likely honors his father R. Meir; after חתנו it may refer back to Rashi. The text as transmitted is ambiguous and has been rendered accordingly."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:46:44.047353209Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}